    /// Installs a charging limit.
    ///
    /// Sends the request to the charging station and blocks the HTTP request
    /// until it answers or ~35s elapses. The station's own accept/reject
    /// decision is never reported as an HTTP error: `201` means it accepted
    /// the limit, which now exists as an addressable resource (`limit` names
    /// it, and `Location` points at it); `200` means the station answered but
    /// rejected it (`accepted: false`, `limit` absent), so nothing was
    /// created. The codes below are for cases the station never got to decide
    /// at all: `409` means it was not connected; `422` means the request
    /// itself would exceed what this station has told the CSMS it can hold,
    /// or the schedule's `recurrence`/`anchor` combination is
    /// self-contradictory; `502` means the station disconnected mid-flight or
    /// answered with something this service could not interpret; `504` means
    /// nothing came back before the deadline; `503` means the request could
    /// not even be sent.
    ///
    /// **`Idempotency-Key` is optional.** Because this call can block for
    /// ~35s, a client that times out and retries would otherwise install the
    /// limit twice. With a key, a retry carrying the same body answers what
    /// the first request answered (the same `201` with the same `Location`
    /// and body, or the same `200` rejection) without contacting the station;
    /// a different body is `409 idempotencyKeyConflict`; and a retry that
    /// arrives while the first request is still waiting on the station is
    /// `409 idempotencyKeyInFlight`.
    ///
    /// **A request the station never got to decide frees its key** — `409
    /// notConnected`, and any other outcome that never put anything on the
    /// wire to the station at all — a retry is attempted exactly as if there
    /// had been no first request. **A request whose outcome is merely unknown
    /// does not**: a `504` timeout, a mid-flight disconnect, or an
    /// unintelligible answer may already have reached the station, so freeing
    /// the key would risk a retry installing a second, duplicate limit next to
